iles. A useful diagnostic line might read printf("x=%d, y=%d\n", x, y); and every comma, quote, slash, and parenthesis matters. A path ending in a backslash, a quoted filename, or an escaped character can all look similar while meaning different things. A useful diagnostic line might read printf("x=%d, y=%d\n", x, y); and every comma, quote, slash, and parenthesis matters. Old computers invite a different kind of attention from modern machines. Their limits are visible, their delays are understandable, and a user can often trace an action from a key press to a program and finally to a device. Pipes connect commands: DIR | SORT sends one program's output into another program instead of directly to the screen. In C, braces group statements: if(ok){count++;} else {errors++;} is tiny, but.
